function Profile({ user }) {
  const [tab, setTab] = useState('posts');

  if (!user) return <p>Loading…</p>;

  const [likes, setLikes] = useState(0);

  return (
    <section>
      <h1>{user.name}</h1>
      <p>{tab}: {likes} likes</p>
    </section>
  );
}

explanation:
React stores a component's hook state as an ordered list and matches each hook call to an entry **by call order**. That is why hooks must be called in the same order on every render.

- First render (`user` is `null`): only `useState('posts')` runs, then the component returns early. React stores **one** hook.
- Second render (`user` is set): this is an update, so React walks the stored list. The first `useState` matches entry 1, but the second `useState` has nothing stored to match, and React throws `Rendered more hooks than during the previous render.` In development React also logs a warning that it detected a change in the order of Hooks.

With no error boundary, an uncaught render error unmounts the whole root, so the page goes blank instead of showing the profile. The opposite direction (rendering with a user first, then `null`) also throws: `Rendered fewer hooks than expected. This may be caused by an accidental early return statement.`

**Fix:** call every hook unconditionally at the top level, before any early return:

```jsx
function Profile({ user }) {
  const [tab, setTab] = useState('posts');
  const [likes, setLikes] = useState(0);

  if (!user) return <p>Loading…</p>;
  // ...
}
```

