async function fetchResults(query) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`);
  return res.json(); // a freshly parsed array on every call
}

function SearchResults({ query }) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  async function load() {
    const data = await fetchResults(query);
    setResults(data);
  }

  useEffect(() => {
    load();
  }, [load]);

  return <ul>{results.map((r) => <li key={r.id}>{r.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}

explanation:
A function declared in the component body is a new function object on every render. Used as an effect dependency, it makes the dependency array different after every commit, so the effect re-runs after every render.

That turns into a cycle here:

- the effect calls `load()`, which fetches
- `setResults(data)` stores a new array (a new reference), so the component re-renders
- the new render creates a new `load`, so `[load]` changed
- the effect runs again and fetches again, forever

Because `setResults` runs after an `await`, each update starts a separate render later on rather than a synchronous chain of nested updates, so React never throws or warns "Maximum update depth exceeded". The page looks fine; the requests just never stop.

Fix it by moving the function into the effect so the effect depends only on `query`:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  async function load() {
    const data = await fetchResults(query);
    setResults(data);
  }
  load();
}, [query]);
```

Alternatively, wrap `load` in `useCallback(..., [query])` so it keeps the same identity until `query` changes. Adding a cleanup that ignores stale responses is still good practice for races when `query` changes, but it is not what stops this loop.

snippets.code:
function Profile({ person }) {
  const [bio, setBio] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchBio(person).then(setBio);
  }, [person]);

  return <p>{person}: {bio ?? 'Loading...'}</p>;
}

explanation:
Responses can arrive in any order, so the last request to **resolve** wins, not the last one **started**. The fix is to make each effect run able to tell whether it is still current.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  fetchBio(person).then(d => {
    if (!ignore) setBio(d);
  });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [person]);
```

- Each run of the effect creates its **own** `ignore` variable, and its `.then` callback and cleanup both close over that same variable.
- When `person` changes from Bob to Carol, React runs Bob's cleanup before Carol's effect. That sets Bob's `ignore` to `true`, and Carol's run gets a fresh `ignore = false`.
- When Bob's slow response finally arrives, his callback sees his own `ignore === true` and skips `setBio`. Only the run for the currently selected person can update state.

The same cleanup also covers unmounting: a response that arrives after `Profile` is removed is ignored. This works the same with or without StrictMode.

If the request API accepts an `AbortSignal` (like `fetch`), you can instead create an `AbortController` in the effect and call `controller.abort()` in the cleanup, which also cancels the outdated request. The `ignore` flag works with any Promise.

