function ShoppingList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState(['Milk']);

  function addBread() {
    items.push('Bread');
    setItems(items);
  }

  return (
    <>
      <ul>
        {items.map((item, i) => <li key={i}>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <button onClick={addBread}>Add Bread</button>
    </>
  );
}

explanation:
When you call a state setter, React compares the new value with the current state using `Object.is`. If they are the same, React skips re-rendering the component and its children, so the DOM is not updated.

`items.push('Bread')` mutates the existing array in place. `setItems(items)` then passes that **same** array object back, so `Object.is(oldItems, newItems)` is `true` and the update is ignored. Each later click does the same thing, so the list never changes.

The data did change, though: the array now really contains the extra `Bread` entries. If something else later re-renders `ShoppingList` (for example, another state update in it), all of them suddenly appear at once, which makes this bug confusing to track down.

Fix: treat state as read-only and pass a **new** array:

```jsx
function addBread() {
  setItems([...items, 'Bread']);
}
```

The same rule applies to objects: `obj.count++; setObj(obj)` is skipped for the same reason; use `setObj({ ...obj, count: obj.count + 1 })` instead.

